    "cultural_tensions": [
      {
        "description": "An institution that produces architectural discourse (Tel Aviv University, presumably including an architecture school) is itself an exemplar of the condition it teaches students to critique — the lecture hall as Junkspace, the university as both critic and perpetrator of spatial banality",
        "friction_type": "internal"
      },
      {
        "description": "The trilinguality of the TAU banner (Hebrew, English, Arabic) performs cosmopolitan institutional legitimacy while the surrounding space — identical blue seats, institutional clock, handrail, acoustic void — performs the opposite: the stripped, evacuated non-place that Koolhaas diagnosed as late capitalism's spatial residue",
        "friction_type": "both"
      }
    ],
    "functional_affects": ["dark humor", "resignation", "mild indignation"],
    "site_resonance": "No site context was injected — the site_context field contains a placeholder. The meme is location-tagged as none specified. However, the image itself is highly site-specific: Tel Aviv University is visually identified by the trilingual institutional banner. The site resonance must therefore be read from image content alone. The spatial condition photographed — a modernist lecture hall of the 1960s–70s Israeli institutional type — is itself the site. The Junkspace diagnosis lands hardest precisely here: a space built to transmit critical architectural thought that has itself become the thing being critiqued. The gap between the absent site context data and the richly legible image is itself a small irony the meme would appreciate.",
    "meme_summary": "The meme turns Koolhaas's own critical vocabulary against the institutional space that teaches it, collapsing the distance between critique and object."
  }

