{
    "pass": 1,
    "rhetorical_moves": ["irony", "satire"],
    "cultural_tensions": [
      {
        "description": "The gap between the official Bauhaus/White City heritage brand — UNESCO-listed, internationally marketed — and the actual built reality of Tel Aviv, where any white-ish flat-roofed building gets absorbed into the label regardless of its actual architectural lineage or condition",
        "friction_type": "external"
      },
      {
        "description": "Local residents and users experience buildings as functional or dysfunctional urban matter; the 'Bauhaus' designation is imposed from above as a cultural-economic label that reframes decay, generic modernism, or speculative construction as heritage — producing a cognitive dissonance between lived texture and official narrative",
        "friction_type": "both"
      }
    ],
    "functional_affects": ["dark humor", "deflation", "wry resignation"],
    "site_resonance": "No specific site was tagged. The meme operates at the scale of Tel Aviv as a whole — it targets the city's identity-branding mechanism rather than a particular location. The site context is therefore the generic Tel Aviv urban fabric itself: the condition where architectural label and architectural reality have drifted so far apart that any random building becomes a credible candidate for misidentification. The absence of a specific location is itself meaningful — the joke depends on the arbitrariness being universal.",
    "meme_summary": "The meme satirizes Tel Aviv's reflexive Bauhaus-labeling culture by staging the archetypal misidentification: any random building, through the logic of the brand, becomes 'possibly Bauhaus,' collapsing the distinction between heritage and generic modernist stock."
  }

